  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nBroad northwest upper flow continues tonight, with flow turning\nmore northerly heading into tomorrow. Weak shortwaves are \ntraversing the flow this evening and overnight, with a chance \nfor light rain showers and even some snow showers. \nGround/pavement temperatures remain widely above freezing in the\n40s north of HWY 2 to near 60 in the far south this afternoon. \nAir temperatures in the mid 30s however are already supportive \nof snow across much of the Lake of the Woods/Red Lakes area with\nshowers (both rain and snow) to overspread much of the forecast\narea this afternoon and persist into tonight. There is a low \n(10%) probability for some freezing drizzle to mix in through \nsaturation looks to generally be deep enough to reach to warm \nend of the DGZ and allow for ice nucleation. Perhaps elevated \nsurfaces could be at and increased risk of icing but otherwise \nimpacts should be minimal. With any snow that falls, rates \nshould be low and not enough to overcome warm roads but some \ngrassy accumulations could be seen from Park rapids to \nGrygla/Roseau and east to the Red Lakes. Would not expect more \nthan 1\" even in higher end scenarios where lake effect processes\noccur downstream of Lake of the Woods and the Red Lakes.\n\nDaytime highs will be in the 30s on Thursday through Saturday. \nOvernight lows fall into the teens Thursday night into Friday \nmorning with wind chills falling into the single digits at times\nin Devils Lake Basin. Aside from the showers today/tonight, dry\nweather is expected to persist through much of the forecast \nperiod. Warmth returns late weekend with highs back in the 50s \nfor the first half of next week.\n\nLooking down the road a bit, there is growing support for a \npotentially active period of weather starting towards the latter\nhalf of next week. This starts with a strong signal for \nblocking across the central CONUS and into most of the Canadian \nPrairies. Resultant troughs form on either side of the mid level\nridge, which eventually dislodge and work eastward. Ensemble \nclusters are beginning to support solutions that hint at \nprecipitation toward the very end of the forecast period.",
